# Copy to `.env` and adjust. `npm run dev` and `npm start` both load this
# file automatically (via node's --env-file-if-exists), and the systemd unit
# reads it through EnvironmentFile= — so the same file works either way. A
# real environment variable always wins over a value set here.
#
# Every setting below is optional: the relay runs on its built-in defaults
# when started from a shell that already has the agent CLI on PATH.

# Absolute path to your agent CLI's binary. Defaults to the bare name
# "claude", resolved via the relay process's own PATH. Renamed from
# `CLAUDE_BIN`, which still works so an existing .env survives an upgrade
# untouched — deprecated, don't use it in a new setup.
# AGENT_BIN=/home/user/.local/bin/claude

# Colon-separated extra directories prepended to the PATH of every spawned
# agent child process — for tools the agent itself invokes (node, git,
# a Node version manager's shim dir...) that wouldn't otherwise be found by
# name alone under systemd.
# EXTRA_PATH_DIRS=/home/user/.local/bin:/home/user/.nvm/versions/node/v20.19.0/bin

# Port and bind address for the relay's HTTP/WebSocket server.
# Defaults to 127.0.0.1 (localhost-only). Setting 0.0.0.0 exposes the relay —
# unauthenticated, running with --dangerously-skip-permissions by default
# (see README "Security model") — to your entire LAN.
# RELAY_PORT=8765
# RELAY_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Where to persist session/background-job state across restarts.
# RELAY_SESSIONS_FILE=/home/user/.anywh-sessions/default.json
# RELAY_BACKGROUND_JOBS_FILE=/home/user/.anywh-sessions/default-bg-jobs.json

# Directory for uploaded files (images pasted/sent from the client).
# RELAY_UPLOAD_DIR=/tmp/anywh-uploads

# Overrides $HOME for the spawned `claude` process — used to run a second,
# fully isolated profile (separate ~/.claude config) alongside the default one.
# RELAY_HOME_OVERRIDE=/home/user/.anywh-work-home

# Absolute path to the `tmux` binary (used by the terminal/tmux session
# feature). Defaults to the standard system location.
# TMUX_BIN=/usr/bin/tmux

# How long (ms) the relay waits for in-flight turns to finish before a
# restart/shutdown forces them closed. Defaults to 4 minutes.
# RELAY_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS=240000

# The model list each agent CLI offers comes from its server and account, so it
# can change without the CLI changing. The relay probes it again once it is
# older than the TTL (default 6 hours, ms), and checks hourly (ms, at least
# 1000) while a client is connected and on each new connection.
# RELAY_MODEL_CATALOG_TTL_MS=21600000
# RELAY_MODEL_CATALOG_REFRESH_MS=3600000

# "Open in editor" from the file panel's context menu (journal/60). Locality
# is declared here, never inferred from the connecting peer's address alone
# — a loopback peer can mean either "same machine" or, in a different
# topology, a proxy tunneled through 127.0.0.1, and a Tailscale-IP peer can
# still be this same physical machine. Both unset (the default) hides the
# feature entirely on the client.
#
# Set when a client connects to this relay FROM THIS SAME MACHINE (typical
# desktop self-host) — lets the client open a file panel path with a local
# deep link (zed://file..., vscode://file...). Has no effect for a client
# connecting from elsewhere: the relay only ever honors this when the
# request's peer address really is this machine's own.
# ANYWH_EDITOR_LOCAL=1

# Set when a client connects to this relay from a DIFFERENT machine
# (see docs/remote-access.md) — lets the client open a file panel path
# via the editor's SSH-remote deep link, dialing this value from the
# client's own machine. Anything `ssh` itself accepts as a target works,
# including a `~/.ssh/config` alias (usually the least friction).
# ANYWH_EDITOR_SSH=user@host
# ANYWH_EDITOR_SSH=user@host:2222
